    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Cao Xueqin began the novel in the 1740s and left it incomplete at his death in 1763 or 1764; it circulated in hand-copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of eighty chapters until Gao E and Cheng Weiyuan printed a 120-chapter edition in 1791 and 1792.</li><li>Written in vernacular Chinese with the dialogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> Mandarin, it follows the fortunes of the Jia clan and the love of Baoyu, Daiyu and Baochai, and is known for its large cast and its female characters.</li><li>Scholars still dispute who wrote the last 40 chapters, and a field of study, Redology, grew up around the novel, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and its lost ending.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> carry comments in red or black ink by unknown commentators, some of whom may have been members of Cao Xueqin's own family; the most prominent, Zhiyanzhai, revealed much about the original ending, which is now lost.</p></aside>
    </section>

